.TH DANBYLAB 2
.SH NAME
danbylab: run, parse, masses, equilibrium, march \- Danby models as scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "draw.m";
include "danbylab.m";
danbylab := load Danbylab Danbylab->PATH;

err := danbylab->run(ctxt, name, spec);
(m, cfg, err) := danbylab->parse(spec);

mass := danbylab->masses(m, cfg);
(x, y) := danbylab->equilibrium(m, cfg);
data := danbylab->march(m, cfg);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Danbylab
is the whole of the Danby viewer \(em window, integrator, history and
controls \(em as a library the model calls with its own script:
.PP
.EX
danbylab->run(ctxt, "threebody", SPEC);
.EE
.PP
Two languages share that script.
.IR Dynsys (2)
reads the equations:
.BR param ,
.BR maxtime ,
.BR state ,
.BR derivative ,
.BR eq ,
.B observable
and
.BR point .
.I Danbylab
reads everything that says what the picture is, and returns it as a
.BR Config .
The split is the design: the model is the equations, the config is the
picture, and
.B parse
returns both and opens no window, so a test can check a model's numbers
where there is no display at all.
.SS The shared vocabulary
.TP
.BI family " name"
Which of the seven families this model belongs to \(em
.BR mechanism ,
.BR population ,
.BR trajectory ,
.BR orbit ,
.BR ecology ,
.B flight
or
.BR profile .
Required, and required first: it chooses the panels, the graph and the
rules a step obeys, and a family's own words are refused in any other.
.TP
.BI title " text..."
The window title.  Required.
.TP
.BI manual " page"
The manual page the window manager's Help menu opens.  A model that
names none is assumed to have a page called
.BI danby- name \fR.
.SS The families
.B Mechanism
draws a linkage beside a two-trace graph.  It needs
.B point
lines, exactly two
.B observable
lines, and at least one
.BR link :
.TP
.BI link " name name"
A rod between two of the model's points.  Repeatable.
.PP
.B Population
draws a stack of bars beside a graph with one trace per state; a click in
a bar sets that state.  It has no words of its own.
.PP
.B Trajectory
is a flight in a vertical plane: four states, a position and its
velocity, and a
.B maxtime
the run ends at, or sooner if the height crosses zero.
.PP
.B Orbit
draws bodies and their trails on a night sky, the most massive one
pinned at the centre of the view.  Four states per body \(em
.IR x ,
.IR y ,
then the two velocity components \(em and exactly two
.B observable
lines, which are the two traces:
.TP
.BI body " name expr"
A body, its label on the diagram, and its mass.  Repeatable.  The mass
is an EXPRESSION over the parameters, not a number, because in this
family a slider usually IS a mass; it is evaluated at every reset.
.PP
.B Ecology
draws a phase portrait beside a time series for exactly two species; a
click in the portrait starts again at the point clicked, and a slider
bends the trajectory that is already running rather than restarting it:
.TP
.BI equilibrium " xexpr " , " yexpr"
Where the two rates vanish, as expressions over the parameters.
Required \(em it is what this family shows.  The comma is a word of its
own, because either half may have spaces in it.
.TP
.BI scale " num"
Pin both phase axes at
.IR num .
Without this they grow to hold the trajectory.
.PP
.B Flight
is a flight in three dimensions: six states \(em downrange, lateral and
height, then their three velocities \(em drawn as a side view above a
plan view.  Unlike the flat families an aim or a crosswind is signed, so
a negative parameter minimum is meaningful.
.PP
.B Profile
is a still picture: a march outward in radius rather than forward in
time, with no clock and no Pause button.  Exactly two
.B observable
lines, which are the two curves:
.TP
.BI radius " startexpr " , " spanexpr"
Where the march starts and how far it runs.  Required.
.TP
.BI samples " n"
How many steps the march takes.  At least two; 320 by default.
.TP
.BI normalise " name"
Divide the named observable by its own outermost value, so a curve that
is an absolute quantity can share an axis with one that is a fraction.
.SS Evaluating what the spec declared
The three families whose extra language is arithmetic evaluate it
through
.IR danbylab ,
so a test can ask for the same numbers the picture shows.
.B Masses
returns the
.B body
masses,
.B equilibrium
the
.B equilibrium
point, and
.B march
the profile as
.IR samples
triples of radius and the two observables.  Each needs the model's
parameters already bound, which
.B dynsys->initialstate()
and
.B dynsys->rhs()
both do, so call one of those first.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/danbylab.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danbylabtest (1),
.IR dynsys (2),
.IR plot (2),
.IR panel (9)
.SH BUGS
A window is a window: everything from
.B run
onward needs
.IR draw (2)
and
.IR tk (2),
and only
.B parse
and the three evaluators run without them.
